# Example 19: NF-kB / IkBa signaling - negative-feedback temporal control (Hoffmann et al. 2002)
# In mammalian cells, nuclear NF-kB (a transcriptional activator) is held in the
# cytoplasm by three IkB isoforms (IkBa, -b, -e). A stimulus (e.g. TNF) activates
# IKK, which degrades IkB, releasing NF-kB to the nucleus, where it transcribes
# inflammatory-response genes - including ikba itself. Newly made IkBa re-enters
# the nucleus and sequesters NF-kB, closing a fast negative-feedback loop.
#
# Measured parameters (Hoffmann, Levchenko, Scott & Baltimore, Science
# 298:1241-1245, 2002, DOI:10.1126/science.1071914):
#   - IkBa = strong negative feedback -> fast turn-off of the NF-kB response
#   - IkBb and IkBe damp the system's oscillatory potential and stabilize the
#     response during longer stimulations
#   - in knockout cells lacking IkBb/IkBe the module oscillates; the model
#     (BioModels BIOMD0000000139) shows bimodal signal processing with respect
#     to stimulus duration - persistent signals selectively activate late genes
#
# HelixLang maps the module to the two-node GRN below (NF-kB activates ikba,
# IkBa inhibits NF-kB). Without the stimulus pulse the GRN rests at the
# unstimulated fixed point (no protein output); with the ~10-tick stimulus pulse
# applied in the Python API example, NF-kB peaks and IkBa feedback returns it to
# baseline: the fast turn-off of Hoffmann et al. (2002).

#promoter name=p_ikba strength=0.4          # NF-kB-responsive ikba promoter
#promoter name=p_nfkb strength=0.6          # NF-kB release gate (IKK-modulated)

#gene name=nfkb promoter=p_nfkb
ATG GCT GCT TAA
#end

#gene name=ikba promoter=p_ikba
ATG GCT GCT GCT TAA
#end

#regulate nfkb -> p_ikba strength=+0.8      # nuclear NF-kB activates ikba
#regulate ikba -> p_nfkb strength=-0.9      # IkBa sequesters NF-kB (negative feedback)

